# Design Decisions & Trade-offs #

**Fixed-size buffers, no dynamic allocation**
Embedded firmware avoids heap allocation to prevent fragmentation and keep
memory usage deterministic. Enforcing that constraint here, even on a host
machine, means this code ports to the ESP32 without redesign.

**1-byte length field (max 255-byte payload)**
Chosen over a 2-byte length for v1: it keeps the entire length space
exhaustively testable and keeps the parser simpler to reason about. A
2-byte length is a straightforward future extension if payloads need to
exceed 255 bytes.

**Not thread-safe (v1)**
Both modules assume single-producer, single-consumer usage. This is
intentional scope control — thread safety matters most once these modules
run as separate FreeRTOS tasks, at which point queues/mutexes are the
right tool, not ad-hoc locking inside the library itself. Revisit when
integrating into the ESP32 project.

**Explicit `bool`/enum return values instead of silent failure**
`ring_buffer_push` returns `false` on overflow instead of overwriting
data; `parser_process_byte` distinguishes "in progress" from "checksum
failed" instead of collapsing both into a boolean. Both choices trade a
little caller-side verbosity for making failure states impossible to
ignore accidentally.
